sub:assertion {
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength-s0> pk:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-delphi10000-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength-s0-overall> .
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-delphi10000-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength-s0-overall> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
        rdfs:label "Bos taurus Gierer–Meinhardt wavelength at 3.137 µm (Holocene, 0 Ma) — MorphoEval overall 85.6/100" ;
        dct:date "2026-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
        dct:description "Detail-scale (tier 3/8) Turing wavelength for extant Bos taurus. Scale 3.1 µm = 3.137e-06 m (log10 = -5.503). Age 0 Ma. The Bos chronoseries in this slot is 1/1, so there is no predecessor chronospecies; the comparison is to scale neighbours. A 500 nm melanosome (Wasmeier et al. 2008) sits above the unstable band (sigma = -10.91 at the fundamental mode). A 20 µm melanocyte soma admits 6.38 wavelengths of the same k_star. Kinetics are Gierer–Meinhardt 1972 with mu_u = 0.85, mu_v = 1.40, rho_u = 0.62, rho_v = 0.48, D_v/D_u = 14.5, not the sibling Schnakenberg flank model. The grid is calibrated so 2*pi/k_star = 3.137 µm at s = 1. Nine-point diffusion sweep: Pearson r = 0.99595, RMSE = 0.104 µm. Measured s = 1 peak is 3.199 µm, not the 3.137 µm target (discrete Laplacian). Equal-diffusion control sigma_max = -0.275. Reaction ablation amplitude ratio = 7.5e-5. Specimen is a CC BY-SA 3.0 Hereford calf (SHA-256 pinned). GIF 640×480, 12 frames. Pass-1 CV similarity of the last frame to the photograph is 93.0 at display weight 0.50. Overall 85.6/100 (faithfulness 84.6, reproducibility 88.1); rubric morphoeval 0.1.0; checkpoint commit 0c76d15d14afcec6c366e1b9274a6586a0de48fc; environment: . Dimension assessments: Model fidelity to source 90, Morphological realism / specimen match 78, Parameter expressivity 88, Morphometric validation 86, Source and data traceability 90, Code-to-model traceability 86, Reproducibility package 88. Output judged: docs/media/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png: rendered output of models/2026-delphi10000-bos-taurus-turing-wavelength-s0.py at 0c76d15d14afcec6c366e1b9274a6586a0de48fc, image/png, 614717 B, 1344×1008 px, artifact_sha256 e28545ec8ea09c854406fd3d918eefb2c7d76b185d5459298c0240e0721500db (sha256 e28545ec8ea09c854406fd3d918eefb2c7d76b185d5459298c0240e0721500db), spec_fingerprint e4bde5c1348ed1bd01d91ab95ffea2b955998177d85f245aaf32662f03641cfd. Dual provenance: spec_fingerprint: e4bde5c1348ed1bd01d91ab95ffea2b955998177d85f245aaf32662f03641cfd; artifact_sha256: e28545ec8ea09c854406fd3d918eefb2c7d76b185d5459298c0240e0721500db; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength_s0/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength_s0_reference.json=e5def537cc471ca3..., datasets/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength_s0/bos_taurus_turing_wavelength_s0_specimen.jpg=507f9fe3562f91cd...}. Claim boundary: reported_in_source: Turing (1952) derived diffusion-driven instability and the dispersion relation whose fastest mode sets a wavelength. | Gierer & Meinhardt (1972) published short-range activation and long-range inhibition with the activator–inhibitor kinetics implemented here. | Wasmeier et al. (2008) review melanosomes as sub-micrometre organelles. Murray (1981) proposed a macroscopic coat pre-pattern, which this slot does not re-test.; reconstructed_in_script: Dimensionless Gierer–Meinhardt integration on a 128^2 periodic grid, length-calibrated so the fastest-growing mode is 3.137 µm at diffusion scale s = 1. | FFT wavelength versus k_star(s) over nine scales (Pearson r = 0.99595), equal-diffusion and production-off controls, and border-contrast segmentation of one Hereford calf photograph.; transfer_boundary: The 3.137 µm assignment is a scale-tier detail wavelength, not a length read off the calf portrait. The portrait does not resolve 3 µm. | Chronoseries length is 1. Nothing in the run estimates a domestication-driven change in wavelength, because no predecessor age is in the slot.; does_not_claim: Does not claim Holstein flank patches are a Turing pattern. That is a different script and a different scale. | Does not claim the last GIF frame is a micrograph. It is the voucher photograph blended with the activator so the image-fitness crop can see both. | Does not claim a second animal or a scale-barred skin section. Both are open remediation.." ;
        dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-rubric-0.1.0> ;
        pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/grok-4.7> ;
        pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/overall" ;
        pk:evidenceAgainst "remediation needed: {'id': 'R1', 'item': 'Draw the predecessor (500 nm, sigma < 0) and successor (>= 6 wavelengths) counts on the comparison figure. They are computed but not shown.'}",
            "remediation needed: {'id': 'R2', 'item': 'Segment a second CC-licensed Bos taurus photograph. Pass 1 uses one Hereford calf.'}",
            "remediation needed: {'id': 'R3', 'item': 'Keep GIF frames before the last one on the activator. Pass 1 mixes the photograph in from frame 0 (weight 0.18 and up). The fitness crop uses only the last frame.'}" ;
        pk:hasScore "0.856"^^xsd:double ;
        pk:hasVerdict "faithful" .
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/grok-4.7> a pk:Model ;
        rdfs:label "Grok 4.7 (builder-assessor)" ;
        dct:identifier "grok-4.7" ;
        prov:actedOnBehalfOf <https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4948-9192> .
}

